      "id": 1,
      "text": "Moscow, Pennsylvania, takes its name from Russia’s former capital, a claim traced back to early settlers who were likely Prussian Lutherans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moscow%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Rev. Peter Rupert, a Lutheran minister, built the first log house in 1830 and is credited with founding the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://moscowboro.com/history-of-moscow/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The former Lackawanna Railroad station now houses a railroad museum and welcomes excursion trains from Steamtown National Historic Site.",
